      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nIn the first two chapters of this section, we learned how to count, organize numbers, and understand the basic idea of limits. Then, we explored how to compare numbers. In this chapter, we will learn how to move between numbers. We suggest reading the two previous chapters before continuing",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we are born, we cannot walk. Our parents carry us. Slowly, we learn to crawl, then to walk, and finally to run. Learning works the same way: we start simple and build up to harder things.\n\nIn this text, we begin our journey into *pre-mathematics*—where we learn basic number ideas through everyday examples. We will focus on addition: learning how to go from one number to the next",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d6a6b284-a060-4a85-bc6c-9d2fe442254c",
                     "text": "González Redondo, Francisco A.",
                     "page": "00:08:20",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_road",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Roads\n\nFigure 1 shows a part of the archaeological site of Teotihuacan in Mexico:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"ChronologyTeotihuacan\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('ChronologyTeotihuacan', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: General view of Teotihuacan. Photograph taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "10",
                     "day": "19"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "General view of Teotihuacan city. In this photo it is possible to observe different constructions in format Talud-Tablero, and in the background we have pyramidal structures representing sacred mountains.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The indigenous peoples of the Americas (*Abya Yala*) built roads inside their cities. They also built long roads to connect different regions. For example, the Mayas made **sacbes** (plural: **sacbeob**)—white roads that made it easier to travel between cities",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "19ea1063-0ddd-4468-ad3e-2635675ca68d",
                     "text": "LaRocque, A., Leblon, B., Ek, J., and Folan, W. J.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2022",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "In the south, the Incas built an amazing road network called **Qhapaq Ñan**. It was 39,000 kilometers long and connected many regions. Some parts were first built by older civilizations, like the Wari",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0bc6455e-7fdb-49cd-bf29-c3675aca4d4f",
                     "text": "Jurado, L. R. C. V.",
                     "page": "pp. 1270 - 1271",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: On Roads</b><br /><br />Roads are not just from the Americas (<i>Abya Yala</i>). Today, we still build streets, highways, and routes to help us move and connect cities.<br /><br />Rivers also serve as natural roads. Many cultures have used and still use rivers as pathways.<br /><br />People build roads with natural materials. Nature creates its own paths too, like rivers. Both can be described with mathematics.<br /><br />Based on what we have learned, can you see any connection between numbers and roads?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In math, we use a similar idea to show movement and position: **axes**. An axis is like a road with marked spots. Numbers show where we are, like steps on a path",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3918f1d9-8d7f-439f-8f76-e5c4ef741b5e",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg width=\"450\" height=\"100\"  fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\"> <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <line x1=\"2\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"15\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <text x=\"0\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0</text> <text x=\"40\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">1</text> <text x=\"80\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">2</text> <text x=\"120\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">3</text> <text x=\"160\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">4</text> <text x=\"200\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">5</text> <text x=\"240\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">6</text> <text x=\"280\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">7</text> <text x=\"320\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">8</text> <text x=\"360\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">9</text> <line x1=\"5\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"5\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"45\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"125\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"245\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"285\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"325\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"325\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"365\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"365\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" />  </svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Gráfico 1</b>: Representation of an axis with numbers. The numbers are placed below the line, and an arrow on the right end indicates the direction in which the numbers grow.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_sum_body",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Rereading the Order of Numbers\n\nBefore we learn about addition, let's review a table from *Drawing to Count*. The **Yupana** was an old Inca counting tool, like an abacus. This table uses the *\"Yupana Inka Tawa Pukllay\"* method (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>), created by Dhavit Prem from the *Yupanki Association*. It is based on the *Yupana* described by Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala (<htcite3>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a62b520a-f443-4d83-ab51-b99c9a46dab8",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "pp. 354 - 363",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2d2ea4a5-506b-4dbf-aaa4-3a10a8fbbd9c",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9618a83e-f232-49db-84f6-fc3455a3f1d1",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Drawing to count",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "f5b74f18-0bc4-46d2-8a5c-64dd80b74dd1",
                     "text": "Poma de Ayala, Felipe Guaman",
                     "page": "pp. 361 - 362",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1615",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"yupana0\" class=\"tawapukllay\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\"><i>Yupana</i></th><th>Left Hand</th><th>Right Hand</th><th>Arrows</th></tr> <tr id=\"tf1\"><td id=\"tc1f1\"><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_up\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_center\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_bottom\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c2_up\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c2_bottom\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc2f1\"><span class=\"dot three_dot_bottom\"></span><span class=\"dot three_dot_up\"></span><span class=\"dot three_dot_center\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc3f1\"><span class=\"dot two_dot_bottom\"></span> <span class=\"dot two_dot_up\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc4f1\"><span class=\"dot dot_center\"></span></td><td id=\"tc5f1\"><img id=\"leftHandImg\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('leftHandImg', '0%')\" /></td><td id=\"tc6f1\"><img id=\"rightHandImg\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('rightHandImg', '0%')\" /></td><td id=\"tc7f1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-up upArrowWithFA\" id=\"traineeUp0\"></i>  <i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-down downArrowWithFA\"style=\"font-size:4em;color:#00f;\" id=\"traineeDown0\"></td></tr></table><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Representation of a  <i>Yupana</i> according to Poma de Ayala (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). CSS inspired by the code of <htcite2>.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a62b520a-f443-4d83-ab51-b99c9a46dab8",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "pp. 355",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "f5b74f18-0bc4-46d2-8a5c-64dd80b74dd1",
                     "text": "Poma de Ayala, Felipe Guaman",
                     "page": "pp. 361 - 362",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1615",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "13"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "48dd9187-7ecc-4f54-a387-d9ecc2497de3",
                     "text": "W3School",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "01",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2018",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1615",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When we use the arrows on the right, we see the fingers go up one by one. This shows the numbers from 0 to 9 in order. Circles in the Yupana show the same numbers",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "f5b74f18-0bc4-46d2-8a5c-64dd80b74dd1",
                     "text": "Poma de Ayala, Felipe Guaman",
                     "page": "pp. 361 - 362",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1615",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a62b520a-f443-4d83-ab51-b99c9a46dab8",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "pp. 354 - 363",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2d2ea4a5-506b-4dbf-aaa4-3a10a8fbbd9c",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Moving fingers up to count is more than just ordering. It is about moving forward by adding one. Each time we raise a finger, we go to the next number by adding 1. We can show this with the \"+\" symbol:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>7</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>7</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>8</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>8</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>9</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Counting by adding 1.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_rules",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### An Axiom of Addition\n\nWhen we study number families, we learn that in the decimal system, we count up to nine (9)—the highest single digit. Then we go back to zero (0) and add one to the digit on the left. Since addition is a way to move between numbers, this rule also works for addition. We can show this using the vertical format:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "00f3ab60-2017-420a-99dc-56de384243e8",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Numerical families (Part I)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "94c4d446-cc89-499b-8404-b528e5107864",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"text-align=center\"><center>&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;9<br />+&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1<br />____________<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;1 0</center></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Adding 1 to 9 in vertical format.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "But Calculation 2 is missing some important details. The number 1 on top looks like it just \"drops\" to the result. The zero (0), which means empty (<htcite0>), is usually hidden on the left side of a number. But we can show it. Let’s rewrite Calculation 2 (<htcite1>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "576e4648-77fa-4a6d-9e51-9459ced964f3",
                     "text": "Musa, Auwalu",
                     "page": "pp. 46 - 48",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "94c4d446-cc89-499b-8404-b528e5107864",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"text-align=center\"><center>&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 9<br />+&nbsp;&nbsp;0 1<br />____________<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;1 0</center></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Adding 1 to 9 in vertical format, showing the hidden zero.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In Calculation 3, the number 1 does not just move to the result. What really happens is 9 + 1 = 10. Since each position can only hold one digit (0–9), the extra value (above nine) moves to the next position. When we see no number on the left, we know there is a hidden zero there",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "In Calculation 3, we used an important math rule—**the identity property of addition**—without saying its name. Let’s stop and look at this simple but powerful idea.\n\n#### What is an axiom?\n\nAn axiom is a basic rule we accept as true without proof. Let’s make this clear with an example of the **addition identity**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7a9c733b-ca13-40f5-8849-19012540b028",
                     "text": "Ernst, Dana.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
